WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.870
Sally Satel: Imagine the worst headache of your life,
multiply it by a thousand, you have it

00:00:03.870 --> 00:00:12.100
every day. For some people, the only
medication that works are opioids.

00:00:19.840 --> 00:00:24.700
Amy Monahan-Curtis: My name is Amy. I'm from Cincinnati Ohio.

00:00:24.700 --> 00:00:28.019
One
day I was walking to class and I simply

00:00:28.019 --> 00:00:32.340
stepped off the curb, came down on my knee
and my hands. And then a couple weeks

00:00:32.340 --> 00:00:42.030
later, I had such intense screaming pain
down my arm. The doctor diagnosed me with

00:00:42.030 --> 00:00:48.360
cervical dystonia. He started me on
regular Botox shots and then medications,

00:00:48.360 --> 00:00:52.700
usually muscle relaxers, and then
sometimes pain medication.

00:00:53.740 --> 00:00:55.420
Anne Fuqua: My name is Anne Fuqua

00:00:55.420 --> 00:01:00.180
and I'm from Birmingham, Alabama.
When I was a child I was originally

00:01:00.180 --> 00:01:03.359
diagnosed with mild cerebral palsy. They said, you know, it wouldn't get any worse, but

00:01:03.359 --> 00:01:07.920
they didn't pick up on the fact that I
had dystonia and not CP at the time. This

00:01:07.920 --> 00:01:12.960
was the point that I first started
taking quote-unquote "pain pills" and

00:01:12.960 --> 00:01:18.960
it was not something that I wanted to do
at all but it helped. Back when I was

00:01:18.960 --> 00:01:24.600
down on lower dose, well completely off
of the opioids, you know, doing something

00:01:24.600 --> 00:01:28.830
like, you know, just standing up like that
would be almost impossible by myself. I

00:01:28.830 --> 00:01:34.320
think what people don't understand is
pain medication is not something that

00:01:34.320 --> 00:01:41.660
takes your pain away. It helps take off a
little edge so that you're just not

00:01:41.660 --> 00:01:45.380
completely screaming.

00:01:47.380 --> 00:01:51.560
Sally Satel: The problem is that
doctors are becoming increasingly

00:01:51.570 --> 00:01:57.420
pressured to reduce prescribing for even
these chronic patients and they either

00:01:57.420 --> 00:02:04.180
taper them off or they discontinue them.
They're told to find other doctors.

00:02:04.180 --> 00:02:10.460
Amy Monahan-Curtis: I've already had one incident where a pharmacist would not fill my doctor's

00:02:10.470 --> 00:02:16.709
prescription for my pain medication. I
went into withdrawal. I had a lot of

00:02:16.709 --> 00:02:22.950
tremors and muscle aches and nausea, and
she refused to fill the prescription the

00:02:22.950 --> 00:02:27.540
doctor filled. And this this is not uncommon at all.

00:02:31.740 --> 00:02:36.800
Much of doctors' fear of prescribing
medications for people in chronic pain

00:02:36.800 --> 00:02:44.760
can be traced to a guideline produced by
the CDC — Centers for Disease Control — in 2016.

00:02:54.520 --> 00:02:58.180
Anne Fuqua: So many people I care about, you know,
have committed suicide and it's just

00:02:58.180 --> 00:03:02.620
such a sad thing. I mean basically back
then I was doing anything possible to

00:03:02.620 --> 00:03:07.329
try to find a doctor and trying to make
the medication I had last as long as it,

00:03:07.329 --> 00:03:11.349
you know, as long as it could. But I had
pretty much decided that, you know, once

00:03:11.349 --> 00:03:15.549
it runs out it runs out. And if I don't
get any, you know, don't get any fluids,

00:03:15.549 --> 00:03:19.900
don't eat anything, don't drink anything,
you know, it would be over pretty quickly. I mean I

00:03:19.900 --> 00:03:24.000
don't want to die I mean I just don't
want to live like that, though.

00:03:30.340 --> 00:03:38.440
Amy Monahan-Curtis: I am scared every single day that the treatment that helps my condition will

00:03:38.440 --> 00:03:44.200
be gone. That every time I have an
appointment will be the day that I'm

00:03:44.200 --> 00:03:49.800
told I won't be helped anymore.

